    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d350">

                <pb facs="0623" n="609" sameAs="frus:frus1949v05/pg609"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.42700 (R)/4–2649: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Kohler</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-04-26T18:00:00+03:00">April 26, 1949—6
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1054. VOA Russian now totally jammed 1800 and 2100 GMT. New jammers began 2100
                    GMT April 24 blanking every frequency except 9700. April 25 all blanked.
                    Airpouching recording IBD. BBC Russian also blanked. Jammers of powerful type
                    previously heard only against radio Madrid. See no hope getting through by
                    increase signal strength since jammers loud enough blank radio Moscow
                        itself.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d350-f1"/> Urgent use more frequencies at once
                    if available in order retain listeners.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d350-f2"/> VOA by
                    far our most important means direct action Soviet people.</p>
                <p>Sent Department 1054, repeated Munich 2.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kohler</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d350-f1" n="1" xml:id="d350-f1-content">
                <p>The Embassy reported in telegram 1063 from Moscow on April 27 that the jamming
                    still continued, and estimated that between 12 and 21 jammers were now available
                    for simultaneous operation in the Moscow area.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d350-f2" n="2" xml:id="d350-f2-content">
                <p>In reply to the Embassy in telegram 272 on April 28 the Department advised that
                    at present it was technically impossible to increase the number of transmitters;
                    but it derived some satisfaction because the jamming would at least force the
                    Soviet Government to concentrate its “radio activity on jamming rather than more
                    productive projects.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
